.TH smi-pki-verify-client-csr "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI PKI Command"
.SH NAME
smi-pki-verify-client-csr \- Check a received client certificate request before signing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-pki-verify-client-csr
.I CSR_FILE CLIENT_NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Checks \fICSR_FILE\fR and prints its subject.
The request self-signature must be valid, which proves the sender holds the matching private key, and its only Common Name must be exactly \fICLIENT_NAME\fR, the identity the CA agreed to, so a client cannot request a certificate in another client's name.
Otherwise the command names the Common Name found and exits with 1.
.PP
Extensions a request asks for, such as CA:TRUE, serverAuth or a subjectAltName, are not checked because they are never used: signing takes every extension from \fBsmi-pki-client-extensions\fR(1).
\fBsmi-pki-do-client-signing\fR(1) runs this check itself; the command is for inspecting a request on its own.
It is a thin command line wrapper over the matching function in the file based PKI library
\fBpki.sh\fR, so each step of building a CA and signing certificates can be run and inspected
on its own. All material is passed as file names; the command fails if the underlying OpenSSL
operation fails.
.SH SEE ALSO
